---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        {
          id: "s1",
          label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage",
        },
        {
          id: "s2",
          label: "Scope & Applicability",
        },
        {
          id: "s3",
          label: "Basic EPS",
        },
        {
          id: "s4",
          label: "Weighted Average Shares",
        },
        {
          id: "s5",
          label: "Diluted EPS – Convertibles",
        },
        {
          id: "s6",
          label: "Diluted EPS – Options",
        },
        {
          id: "s7",
          label: "Anti-Dilution",
        },
        {
          id: "s8",
          label: "Restatement & Discontinued Ops",
        },
        {
          id: "s9",
          label: "Disclosure Requirements",
        },
        {
          id: "s10",
          label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates",
        },
        {
          id: "s11",
          label: "IFRS vs US GAAP",
        },
        {
          id: "s12",
          label: "Statement Mapping",
        },
        {
          id: "s13",
          label: "Related Standards",
        },
        {
          id: "s14",
          label: "Quick-Reference Glossary",
        },
        {
          id: "history",
          label: "History & Development",
        },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Why a single per-share number needs its own standard</div>
        <div class="principle">EPS is one of the most widely used performance metrics by investors – the Conceptual Framework's decision-usefulness objective demands this figure be calculated CONSISTENTLY across entities, since even small methodological differences would badly distort comparability, one of the Framework's central qualitative characteristics.</div>
        <div class="elabel">HOW IAS 33 MAPS TO THE FRAMEWORK</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span><strong style="font-weight:600">Comparability</strong> – a prescribed, uniform calculation methodology (not a policy choice) ensures EPS figures are meaningfully comparable across entities and over time</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:600">Faithful representation</strong> – DILUTED EPS exists specifically to show the WORST CASE (most dilutive) scenario, preventing an overly optimistic picture of per-share earnings power</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:600">Prudence</strong> – only instruments that would DECREASE EPS (dilutive) are included in diluted EPS; anti-dilutive instruments are excluded, ensuring diluted EPS is never accidentally overstated</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IAS 33 is almost entirely a CALCULATION standard – like IAS 24, it does not change recognition or measurement of profit itself; it prescribes exactly HOW to divide reported profit by a share count to produce a comparable metric.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">The core measurement logic</div>
        <div class="principle">EPS measurement is fundamentally a fraction – the numerator (earnings) and denominator (shares) must each be defined with precision to prevent manipulation or inconsistency.</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>EPS type</span><span>Purpose</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Basic EPS</span><span>Actual, current per-share earnings attributable to ordinary shareholders</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Diluted EPS</span><span>The MAXIMUM potential dilution from all convertible/option instruments – the worst-case, most conservative per-share figure</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info callout--chip" style="margin-bottom:0;"><strong style="font-weight:600">Qualitative characteristics in action:</strong> presenting BOTH basic and diluted EPS with EQUAL prominence reflects <strong style="font-weight:600">neutrality</strong> – users see both the current position and the potential downside from dilutive instruments, without either figure being emphasised over the other.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What IAS 33 covers</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 33 applies to entities whose ordinary shares or potential ordinary shares are PUBLICLY TRADED (or which are in the process of issuing such shares in public markets) – presenting basic and diluted EPS on the face of the statement of profit or loss.</div>
        <div class="elabel">MANDATORY PRESENTATION LOCATION</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">EPS is presented on the FACE of the statement of profit or loss, for profit/loss from CONTINUING operations attributable to ordinary equity holders, AND for profit/loss attributable to ordinary equity holders overall (i.e. including discontinued operations, if any) – with EQUAL prominence given to basic and diluted figures.</div>
        <div class="note">An entity that does NOT have publicly traded shares, but chooses to disclose EPS voluntarily, must still calculate it in accordance with IAS 33 if it makes that choice – there is no "partial compliance" option.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions you must know</div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Ordinary share</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">An equity instrument SUBORDINATE to all other classes of equity instruments.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Potential ordinary share</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">A financial instrument or other contract that may entitle its holder to ordinary shares (e.g. convertible bonds, convertible preference shares, share options, warrants).</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Dilution</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">A REDUCTION in EPS, or an INCREASE in loss per share, resulting from the assumption that convertible instruments are converted, options/warrants are exercised, or ordinary shares are issued upon satisfaction of specified conditions.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;">
          <div class="glossary-term">Antidilution</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">An INCREASE in EPS, or a REDUCTION in loss per share, resulting from the assumption that convertible instruments are converted, options are exercised, or ordinary shares are issued upon satisfaction of specified conditions – such instruments are EXCLUDED from diluted EPS.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Basic EPS – The Core Formula</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">The basic EPS formula</div>
        <div class="formula-box formula-box--chip">
          <div class="f-eq">Basic EPS = Profit/loss attributable to ordinary equity holders of the parent ÷ Weighted average number of ordinary shares outstanding during the period</div>
        </div>
        <div class="elabel">NUMERATOR ADJUSTMENTS</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Profit/loss attributable to ordinary shareholders is calculated AFTER deducting: preference dividends (whether or not declared, if the preference shares are CUMULATIVE), and any other items relating to non-ordinary equity holders.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Worked example – basic EPS with preference dividends</div>
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 1: Deducting cumulative preference dividends</div>
          <div class="wk-body">
            A company's profit for the year attributable to the parent is $3,200,000. It has 500,000 cumulative preference shares in issue with an annual dividend entitlement of $2 per share, none of which was declared this year due to cash constraints. Weighted average ordinary shares outstanding: 2,000,000.
          </div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            <strong>Preference dividend deducted</strong> = 500,000 × $2 = $1,000,000 (deducted REGARDLESS of non-declaration, since the shares are cumulative)<br>
            <strong>Earnings attributable to ordinary shareholders</strong> = 3,200,000 − 1,000,000 = $2,200,000<br>
            <strong>Basic EPS</strong> = 2,200,000 ÷ 2,000,000 = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);">$1.10 per share</strong>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--chip" style="margin-bottom:0;">If the preference shares were NON-cumulative instead, only the dividend ACTUALLY DECLARED for the period would be deducted – a frequently tested distinction.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Weighted Average Shares – Bonus Issues &amp; Rights Issues</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Bonus (capitalisation) issues – retrospective adjustment</div>
        <div class="text-supporting" style="margin-bottom:9px;">A bonus issue (shares issued to existing shareholders for NO consideration) is treated as if it had occurred at the BEGINNING of the EARLIEST period presented – since no new resources are raised, the weighted average share count for ALL periods presented is adjusted retrospectively by the bonus ratio, and PRIOR PERIOD EPS FIGURES ARE RESTATED accordingly.</div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 2: 1-for-4 bonus issue</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A company had 4,000,000 shares in issue throughout 20X1, reporting EPS of $0.80. During 20X2, it makes a 1-for-4 bonus issue (1,000,000 new shares), giving 5,000,000 shares in issue for the rest of the year.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            <strong>20X1 EPS restated</strong> = 0.80 ÷ 1.25 (the bonus factor, 5,000,000/4,000,000) = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);">$0.64</strong> – restated for comparability, even though 20X1's actual profit is unchanged.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Rights issues – the bonus element adjustment</div>
        <div class="text-supporting" style="margin-bottom:9px;">A rights issue often includes a BONUS ELEMENT, since new shares are typically issued below full market price. The weighted average number of shares is adjusted using a THEORETICAL EX-RIGHTS PRICE (TERP) factor to reflect this embedded bonus.</div>
        <div class="formula-box formula-box--chip">
          <div class="f-label">TERP FORMULA</div>
          <div class="f-eq">TERP = (Shares before rights × Fair value before rights + New shares issued × Rights issue price) ÷ Total shares after the rights issue</div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">The adjustment factor (fair value per share before the rights issue ÷ TERP) is applied to shares outstanding BEFORE the rights issue, to reflect the bonus element embedded in the discounted price.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Diluted EPS – Convertible Instruments</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">The "as if converted" method</div>
        <div class="principle">For diluted EPS, the numerator is adjusted by adding back any interest/dividend expense recognised in the period related to potential ordinary shares (net of tax), and the denominator is adjusted by adding the number of ordinary shares that WOULD be issued on conversion – assuming conversion at the BEGINNING of the period (or date of issue, if later).</div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 3: Diluted EPS with a convertible bond</div>
          <div class="wk-body">
            A company reports basic EPS on profit of $2,200,000 over 2,000,000 weighted average shares (basic EPS = $1.10). It also has a convertible bond outstanding all year, with after-tax interest expense of $180,000, convertible into 300,000 ordinary shares.
          </div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            <strong>Adjusted earnings</strong> = 2,200,000 + 180,000 (interest saved, net of tax, if converted) = $2,380,000<br>
            <strong>Adjusted shares</strong> = 2,000,000 + 300,000 = 2,300,000<br>
            <strong>Diluted EPS</strong> = 2,380,000 ÷ 2,300,000 = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);">$1.03 per share</strong> (LOWER than basic EPS of $1.10, confirming genuine dilution)
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Since $1.03 < $1.10, the convertible bond IS dilutive and must be included – see Section 7 for the anti-dilution test that would EXCLUDE an instrument if the reverse were true.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Diluted EPS – Options, Warrants &amp; the Treasury Stock Method</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">The treasury stock method – with worked example</div>
        <div class="principle">Options and warrants are assumed exercised only if they would be DILUTIVE (i.e. the exercise price is BELOW the average market price of shares during the period – "in the money"). The assumed proceeds from exercise are treated as if used to REPURCHASE shares at the average market price – only the NET incremental shares (shares issued minus shares assumed repurchased) are added to the diluted EPS denominator.</div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 4: Share options diluted via the treasury stock method</div>
          <div class="wk-body">
            A company has 100,000 share options outstanding all year, with an exercise price of $4.00. The average market price of the shares during the year was $5.00.
          </div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            <strong>Assumed proceeds on exercise</strong> = 100,000 × $4.00 = $400,000<br>
            <strong>Shares assumed repurchased at average market price</strong> = 400,000 ÷ 5.00 = 80,000 shares<br>
            <strong>Net incremental shares added to the diluted EPS denominator</strong> = 100,000 − 80,000 = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);">20,000 shares</strong> (NOT the full 100,000)
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--chip" style="margin-bottom:0;">Only the NET incremental 20,000 shares are added – a very common calculation error is adding the FULL 100,000 options rather than applying the treasury stock method's net-share logic.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Anti-Dilution – The Instrument-by-Instrument Test</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Testing each potential ordinary share separately, most dilutive first</div>
        <div class="principle">Potential ordinary shares are treated as DILUTIVE only when their conversion to ordinary shares would DECREASE EPS (or increase loss per share) from continuing operations. Instruments are ranked from MOST to least dilutive and included ONE AT A TIME, stopping as soon as adding the next instrument would INCREASE EPS (making it anti-dilutive) – antidilutive instruments are simply excluded from that period's diluted EPS.</div>
        <div class="callout danger callout--chip" style="margin-bottom:0;"><strong style="font-weight:600">An anti-dilutive instrument is NEVER included in diluted EPS, even if basic EPS shows a loss</strong> – the test is applied period by period, and different instruments may be dilutive in some periods and anti-dilutive in others, depending on earnings and share price movements.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Restatement &amp; Discontinued Operations</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Retrospective restatement events</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Bonus issues, share splits (retrospectively adjusted, no restatement of prior PROFIT figures needed – only the share count)</span>
          <span>Prior period error corrections and changes in accounting policy under IAS 8 – EPS is restated for these consistently with the restatement of the underlying financial statements</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Discontinued operations – separate presentation</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">If an entity presents a discontinued operation (IFRS 5), basic and diluted amounts per share for the DISCONTINUED operation are presented either on the face of the SOPL or in the notes – in ADDITION to the continuing-operations and total EPS figures required on the face of the statement.</div>
        <div class="note">This gives users THREE distinct EPS pairs to work with: continuing operations, discontinued operations, and total – a frequently tested presentation nuance.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Required disclosures</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">RECONCILIATION DISCLOSURES</div>
            <div class="check-list">
              <span>The amounts used as the numerators in calculating basic and diluted EPS, and a reconciliation of those amounts to profit/loss attributable to the parent</span>
              <span>The weighted average number of ordinary shares used as the denominator, and a reconciliation of these denominators to each other</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">OTHER DISCLOSURES</div>
            <div class="check-list">
              <span>Instruments that could potentially dilute basic EPS in the future but were EXCLUDED from the diluted EPS calculation because they were anti-dilutive in the current period</span>
              <span>A description of ordinary share transactions or potential ordinary share transactions, OTHER than those already reflected in the reported figures, occurring AFTER the reporting period that would have changed the number of shares significantly had they occurred before year end</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where management judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Whether a specific instrument's terms make it genuinely dilutive or anti-dilutive in a given period</span>
              <span>Determining the correct order in which multiple potential ordinary share instruments should be tested (most dilutive first)</span>
              <span>Assessing whether contingently issuable shares' conditions have genuinely been satisfied at the reporting date</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Average market price of ordinary shares during the period, used in the treasury stock method for options/warrants</span>
              <span>The theoretical ex-rights price (TERP) calculation for a rights issue's bonus element</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--chip" style="margin-top:8px;margin-bottom:0;">EPS is one of the most closely watched metrics by markets and analysts – even seemingly minor errors in the weighted average share calculation (e.g. forgetting to retrospectively adjust for a bonus issue) can materially mislead year-on-year trend analysis.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>IFRS vs US GAAP – Key Differences</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison – IAS 33 vs US GAAP (ASC 260)</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IAS 33)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Core methodology (basic/diluted, treasury stock method)</span><span>As described above</span><span>Very closely converged – the two standards were substantially harmonised through a joint IASB/FASB project</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Contingently convertible instruments</span><span>Included in diluted EPS if dilutive under the general rules, regardless of whether the contingency has been met</span><span>Historically had specific "contingent conversion" guidance requiring inclusion of certain instruments regardless of whether the market price trigger was met – a notable prior divergence, though convergence has narrowed differences over time</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Presentation location</span><span>On the face of the SOPL, with equal prominence for basic/diluted</span><span>Also on the face of the income statement, broadly converged</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Two-class method (participating securities)</span><span>Less explicitly codified as a distinct named method, though similar substance-based principles apply through the general EPS framework</span><span>Explicitly codified "two-class method" required for participating securities that share in dividends with ordinary shares</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IAS 33 and ASC 260 are among the MOST converged pairs of standards in the entire IFRS/US GAAP comparison, following a dedicated joint standard-setting project – remaining differences are narrow and largely technical rather than conceptual.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping – Where Every EPS Component Lands</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Comprehensive event-to-statement mapping table</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event / transaction</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Basic EPS (continuing operations, total)</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL – face</span></span><span>Mandatory presentation with equal prominence to diluted EPS</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Diluted EPS (continuing operations, total)</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL – face</span></span><span>Never higher than basic EPS (or less negative, for a loss)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Discontinued operations EPS</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL – face or Notes</span></span><span>Presented separately alongside continuing/total EPS</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Bonus issue / share split</span><span><span class="badge be">Equity</span></span><span>Retrospective adjustment to weighted average shares for ALL periods presented; prior EPS restated</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Rights issue with bonus element</span><span><span class="badge be">Equity</span></span><span>TERP factor applied to shares before the rights issue</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Convertible bond interest add-back (diluted EPS)</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL (memorandum calc.)</span></span><span>Net-of-tax interest added back to the numerator for diluted EPS purposes only</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Options/warrants (treasury stock method)</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL (memorandum calc.)</span></span><span>Only net incremental shares added to the diluted denominator</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Anti-dilutive instruments</span><span>–</span><span>Excluded from diluted EPS entirely; disclosed as a note</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Numerator/denominator reconciliations</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Mandatory disclosure</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards – Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">How IAS 33 connects across the IFRS body of literature</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 1</span><span>Mandates the presentation LOCATION for basic/diluted EPS on the face of the statement of profit or loss</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 32</span><span>Determines whether a compound instrument's convertible feature (equity component) is relevant potential ordinary shares for diluted EPS purposes</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IFRS 5</span><span>Requires separate presentation of discontinued operations' EPS alongside continuing and total EPS</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IFRS 2</span><span>Employee share options accounted for under IFRS 2 are treated as potential ordinary shares, tested via the treasury stock method for dilution</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 8</span><span>Governs restatement of prior period EPS figures for retrospective policy changes and error corrections</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 10</span><span>Post-year-end share transactions (before authorisation for issue) affecting the ordinary share count require disclosure, even if not reflected in the reported weighted average calculation</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IFRS 3</span><span>Contingently issuable shares from a business combination's consideration arrangements are treated as potential ordinary shares once contingency conditions are met</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Weighted average number of ordinary shares", definition: "The number of shares outstanding at the START of the period, adjusted by shares bought back or issued during the period, weighted by the time they were outstanding." },
      { term: "Treasury stock method", definition: "The method used to calculate the dilutive effect of options and warrants – assumed proceeds from exercise are treated as used to repurchase shares at the average market price, adding only the NET incremental shares to the diluted EPS denominator." },
      { term: "Theoretical ex-rights price (TERP)", definition: "The theoretical share price immediately after a rights issue, calculated as a weighted average of the pre-rights price and the discounted rights issue price, used to identify the bonus element embedded in a rights issue." },
      { term: "Contingently issuable shares", definition: "Ordinary shares issuable for little or no cash/other consideration upon satisfaction of specified conditions – included in diluted EPS if the conditions would be met if the period ended today." },
      { term: "Cumulative preference shares", definition: "Preference shares whose dividend entitlement, if unpaid, accumulates and must be deducted from earnings for EPS purposes REGARDLESS of whether it was actually declared." },
      { term: "Bonus element", definition: "The implicit value given to existing shareholders in a bonus issue or a rights issue priced below fair value, requiring a retrospective adjustment to the weighted average share count." }
    ]} />

